\maketitle

\blfootnote{\Addresses}

\newpage

\section*{Abstract}
This tutorial article provides a comprehensive, step-by-step illustration
of consistent partial least squares (PLSc) using the \pkg{cSEM} package in \textsf{R}.
PLSc is a variant of partial least squares path modeling, a composite-based approach to structural equation modeling, that produces consistent parameter estimates for latent variable models.
In particular, PLSc corrects construct correlations for
attenuation using Dijkstra-Henseler's composite reliability coefficient $\varrho_A$.
Building on the employee retention example
introduced by \citet{Cheah2026}, we demonstrate how to install and load
\pkg{cSEM}, specify the model using \pkg{lavaan}-style syntax, estimate the
PLSc-SEM model, evaluate measurement model quality (internal consistency
reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity), and assess the
structural model (collinearity, effect sizes, model fit, and path
coefficients with bootstrap inference). We further contrast the PLSc-SEM
results with standard PLS-SEM estimates and discuss practical
considerations for using PLSc-SEM in empirical research.


Keywords: measurement error, bias correction, partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), factor score regression


\newpage

\tableofcontents

\pagenumbering{arabic}
% \doublespacing

\section{Introduction}

Structural equation modeling (SEM) is a versatile multivariate analysis framework used in the behavioral, social, medical, and management sciences to study complex relationships between constructs and their relations with observed variables \citep[e.g.,][]{Marcoulides1996,Kline2023}.
To estimate structural equation models, various approaches have been proposed, including maximum-likelihood \citep{Joereskog1970}, weighted least squares \citep{Browne1974}, factor score regression \citep{Skrondal2001,Devlieger2017} and (integrated) generalized structured component analysis \citep{Hwang2004,Hwang2021a}.

A composite-based estimator to SEM that gained popularity over the last decades in various fields of social sciences \citep{Cook2023}, including marketing \citep{Sarstedt2022a}, information systems research \citep{Benitez2020,Evermann2021}, human resource management \citep{Ringle2020}, and tourism research \citep{Mueller2018}, is partial least squares path modeling \citep[PLS-PM,][]{Wold1982c}, also known as partial least squares structural equation modeling \citep[PLS-SEM,][]{Hair2011}.
PLS-PM is a two-step estimation procedure \citep{Schuberth2023d}.
In the first step, construct scores are obtained by the partial least squares algorithm.
Subsequently, these construct scores are used to estimate the model parameters.
Although PLS-PM is computationally efficient, it is known that in its original form to produce inconsistent estimates for models involving latent variables due to attenuation bias \citep[e.g.,][]{Hui1982,Dijkstra1985,Roenkkoe2013,Schuberth2023}.

To address this limitation, consistent partial least squares \citep[PLSc,][]{Dijkstra2013,Dijkstra2014a,Dijkstra2015,Dijkstra2015a} was introduced.
PLSc is based on PLS-PM, but applies a correction for attenuation to obtain consistent estimates of reflective measurement models and path coefficient between latent variables.
Additionally, PLSc has been extended to deal with ordinal variables \citep{Schuberth2018a}, randomly occurring outliers \citep{Schamberger2020} and multicollinearity issues \citep{Jung2018}.

To apply PLS-PM and PLSc, various implementations have been developed \citep{Venturini2023}.
They can be distinguished into: (i) proprietary software and (ii) open-source software.
Proprietary software %is characterized
% Examples of closed-source proprietary software
for conducting PLS-PM and PLSc includes SmartPLS \citep{Ringle2024}, WarpPLS \citep{Kock2025} and ADANCO \citep{adanco}.
On the other hand, open-source software alternatives include implementations in the statistical programming environment R \citep{R} such as matrixpls \citep{Roenkkoe2022a}, SEMinR \citep{seminr}, \pkg{cSEM} \citep{csem} and plspm \citep{Sanchez2025}.
Similarly, it includes implementations in python \citep{python3} such as plspm \citep{Humble2024}.
While proprietary software such as SmartPLS often shows high ease of use \citep{Cheah2024}, they conflict with the open science principles \citep{UNESCO}.
For example, their codebase is not openly accessible, which impedes transparency \citep{Morin2012a}, reproducibility \citep{Ince2012}, and accessibility/inclusiveness \citep{}.
Open-source software overcomes these limitations \citep{Barba2022}.
Therefore, this tutorial presents the R package \pkg{cSEM} as a full-fledged open-source alternative to SmartPLS to apply PLS-PM and PLSc.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\pkg{cSEM}}{cSEM} R Illustration}
\label{sec:illustration}
\subsection{Getting started with \pkg{cSEM}}

To use the \pkg{cSEM} package, users first need to install \textsf{R} and an integrated development environment (IDE) such as \textsf{RStudio}.
For the installation, refer to the official guides for \textsf{R} (\url{https://cran.r-project.org}) and \textsf{RStudio} (\url{https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop}).

When \textsf{RStudio} is opened for the first time, it displays four panes (Figure~\ref{fig:rstudio}).
The two most important ones are the \emph{script editor} (upper left), where code is written and saved, and the \emph{Console} (lower left), where the code is executed and results are printed.
The \emph{Environment} pane (upper right) lists the objects currently loaded into memory, and the fourth pane (lower right) provides access to files, plots, and help pages.

Although code can be typed directly into the console, it is good practice to work in a script, since a script can be saved, shared, and re-run, which is essential for reproducible analyses.
To create a new script, click \textsf{File~$\rightarrow$ New File~$\rightarrow$ R Script}.
The typical workflow is to write code in the editor and run the selected line(s) with \texttt{Ctrl~+~Enter}.
Any line beginning with \texttt{\#} is treated as a comment: it documents what the code does and is ignored when the code runs.

<<label = intro-comment>>=
# This is a comment and is not executed.
1 + 1   # Code is executed; the result is printed in the Console.
@

Before \pkg{cSEM} can be used, it must be installed. The \pkg{cSEM} package is available on CRAN, so the most recent stable version can be installed with:

<<install-csem, eval = FALSE >>=
install.packages("cSEM")
@

Installation only needs to be done once per machine (rerun this line to update the package later).
Moreover, development versions can be installed from GitHub: \url{https://github.com/FloSchuberth/cSEM}.
After installation, the package must be loaded at the start of every new \textsf{R} session before its functions become available:

<<load-csem, message=FALSE>>=
library(cSEM)
@

With \pkg{cSEM} installed and loaded, the data can now be imported and the model specified, as described in the following sections.

\input{LaTeX/plots/rstudio_test.tex}

\subsection{Model and Data}
\label{sec:model-data}
The \pkg{cSEM} package \citep{csem} offers a variety of composite-based approaches to SEM including PLS-PM and PLSc.
Following \citet{Cheah2026}, our illustration of the application of PLSc using \pkg{cSEM} draws on the employee retention model introduced by \citet[Chapter~13]{Hair2019c}. 

The model explains the effects of organizational commitment~(OC) and job satisfaction~(JS) on employees' staying intentions~(SI), with work environment perceptions~(EP) and attitudes toward coworkers~(AC) as exogenous antecedent constructs.
Five constructs are reflectively measured by a total of 21 indicators.
A detailed description of the indicators can be found in \citet{Cheah2026}.
The model is presented in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{"LaTeX/plots/retention_model_preview.png"}
    \caption{Employee Retention Model}
    \label{fig:retentionmodel}
\end{figure}

The dataset used for model estimation is publicly available from the SmartPLS website: \url{https://www.smartpls.com/documentation/sample-projects/employee-retention}. 
The synthetic dataset comprises $N = 400$ observations from HBAT Industries and was generated to satisfy the assumptions of factor-based SEM, including multivariate normality.
For this tutorial, the data is assumed to be downloaded as a CSV file.
After downloading the dataset, it needs to be loaded into the R Environment.
The Employee Retention dataset is stored as a CSV file using ';' as the separator, thus it can be loaded into R using the following command:

<<load-data>>=
# Import dataset
HBAT_SEM <- read.csv("data/HBAT_SEM.csv", sep=";")

# Select relevant variables
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM[, c("AC1", "AC2", "AC3", "AC4",
                   "EP1", "EP2", "EP3", "EP4",
                   "JS1", "JS2", "JS3", "JS4", "JS5",
                   "OC1", "OC2", "OC3", "OC4",
                   "SI1", "SI2", "SI3", "SI4")]

# show dimensions of the relevant dataset
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)

# count missing values per variable
colSums(is.na(HBAT_SEM_rel))
@

Since \pkg{cSEM} can only handle complete datasets, i.e., datasets without any missing values, the dataset is checked using the \fn{is.na()} function.
The output shows that the variables AC4 and EP4 each contain one missing value.
%Before we can continue, we thus need to either apply imputation methods or delete the incomplete observations.
To ensure that the estimation can be perfomed the missing observations need to be deleted or inputation methods need to be applied. 
Deleting incomplete observations can be done via the following command:

<<missing-data>>=
# filter the dataset, only select complete cases
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM_rel[complete.cases(HBAT_SEM_rel),]

#  the number of rows changed from 400 to 398 
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)
@

Since two observations contain missing values, all analyses in the remainder of this tutorial are based on the $N = 398$ complete observations.
When using their own data, users must ensure it is stored as a data frame or a matrix before passing it to \pkg{cSEM} functions; if necessary, the data can be converted with \fn{as.data.frame()} or \fn{as.matrix()}.

\subsection{Model Specification}
In \pkg{cSEM}, models are specified using the \pkg{lavaan} model syntax.
The \pkg{lavaan} package does not need to be installed to use the syntax. 
In particular, the \code{=\textasciitilde} and the \code{<\textasciitilde} operators are used to define the relations between the constructs and their indicators.
Specifically, the \code{=\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify reflective measurement models, while the \code{<\textasciitilde} is used to specify composite models \citep[see][for an explanation of reflective and composite models]{}.
In addition, the \code{\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify the relations among constructs.
In case, an observed variable should be directly accounted for in the structural model, it must be specified as a single indicator construct, as common in PLS-PM \citep{}.
The \pkg{cSEM} specification of the retention model illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel} is as follows:

<<model-spec>>=
model <- "
  # Measurement model
  AC =~ AC1 + AC2 + AC3 + AC4
  EP =~ EP1 + EP2 + EP3 + EP4
  JS =~ JS1 + JS2 + JS3 + JS4 + JS5
  OC =~ OC1 + OC2 + OC3 + OC4
  SI =~ SI1 + SI2 + SI3 + SI4

  # Structural model
  JS ~ EP + AC
  OC ~ EP + AC + JS
  SI ~ JS + OC
"
@

\subsection{Model Estimation}
\label{sec:estimation}

The central function is \fn{csem()}.
The minimal input required for the \fn{csem()} function is a dataset and a model. 
The dataset should be a data frame or a matrix with column names matching the indicator names used in the model.
The package cSEM handles a lot of different data types natively, such as  "\code{logical}", "\code{numeric}" ("\code{double} or "\code{integer}"), "\code{factor}" ("\code{ordered} and/or "\code{unordered}"), "\code{character}" or a mix of several types.
The model is needed to be written in \pkg{lavaan} syntax, with \code{=\~}  for measurement relations and \texttt{\~} for structural (regression) relations.

By default weights are estimated using the consistent partial least squares path modeling algorithm ("PLS-PM").

To ultimately estimate with the csem function, the function is called and save the output is saved as \code{res\_csem}:

Inference and confidence intervals can be obtained via bootstrap resampling. 
For this the parameter \prm{.resample\_method} is set to "bootstrap", another option is "jackknife". 
Confidence intervals are calculated based on \prm{.R} bootstrap resamples, and a \prm{.seed} can be set to ensure reproducibility.

<<csem-est>>=
# PLSc-SEM estimation
res_csem <- csem(
  .data             = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model            = model,
  .resample_method  = "bootstrap",
  .R                = 1000,
  .seed             = 42
)
@

To obtain a comprehensive overview of all results, including loadings, path coefficients, reliability measures, and $R^2$ values, use:

<<summary-rescsem, excerpt.lines = 8>>=
# Full results summary
summary_res <- summarize(res_csem)
summary_res
@
The ouput above has been shortened for readability. 
The "\dots" show omitted lines.
Executing the provided code will return the whole output.

\section{Assessment Methods}
\label{sec:assessment}

Next, the model estimates are assessed, according to the guidelines described in \citet{Benitez2020}.
This assessment comprises three parts: 
The assessment of the overall model fit, the assessment of the reflective measurement models, and the evaluation of the structural model.

\subsection{Overall Model Fit}
\label{sec:overall-fit}
Before the overall model fit can be tested, the estimation needs to be validated. 
This is done with the \fn{verify()} function. 
This function gives the user details about the estimation. 
It gives information if convergence is achieved, all absolute standardized loading 
estimates are less than or equal to one.
It also checks whether the construct variance covariance matrix is positive semi-definite, if all reliability estimates are less than or equal to one and if the model-implied indicator variance covariance matrix is positive semi-definite.
If a check fails, the results should not be interpreted and the user needs to customize the algorithm to ensure a valid estimation process.
For that see Section \ref{sec:customization}.
<<verify>>=
# Check whether the estimation is admissible
verify(res_csem)
@

After that the overall model fit with the function \fn{testOMF()} can be examined.
The function \fn{testOMF()} is a bootstrap-based test for model fit originally proposed by \citet{Beran1985}.
See also \citet{Dijkstra2015} who first suggested the test in the context of PLS-PM.
By default \fn{testOMF()} tests the null hypothesis that the population indicator correlation matrix equals the population model-implied indicator correlation matrix. 
\fn{testOMF()} uses different distance measures to assess the distance between the sample indicator correlation matrix and the estimated model-implied indicator correlation matrix, such as the geodesic distance $d_G$, the standardized root mean square residual $SRMR$, the squared Euclidean distance $d_L$ and the distance based on the maximum likelihood fit function $d_{ML}$. 
The reference distribution for each test statistic is obtained via bootstrap with \prm{.R} resamples as proposed by \citet{Beran1985}.
Reproducibility is ensured through the parameter \prm{.seed}.
<<test-omf>>=
# Bootstrap-based test of the overall model fit
fit_test <- testOMF(
  res_csem,
  .R                    = 1000,
  .seed                 = 42,
  .handle_inadmissibles = "replace"
)
fit_test
@

\subsection{Assessment of the Reflective Measurement Models}
\label{sec:measurement}
The next step is about assessing the reflective measurement models.
%The assessment follows the guideline described in \citet{Benitez2020}. 
It includes construct reliability, indicator reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity.
The \fn{assess()} function computes all relevant assessment criteria.
The output shows all criteria grouped in titled sections. 
The output here is shortened for presentation.
The "\dots" mark omitted lines.
Running the code gives the full report.

<<assess-quality, excerpt.lines = 5, warning = FALSE>>=
#Compute all quality criteria
quality <- assess(res_csem)
quality
@

In the following, the criteria of \fn{assess()} are extracted and explained one at a time.

\subsubsection*{Evaluating Construct Reliability}
\label{sec:reliability}

In this step we evaluate whether the items reliably represent the underlying construct.
A construct with high reliability tends to produce similar items under consistent conditions \citet{Reliability}.
The \pkg{cSEM} package offers a variety of different reliability coefficients.
These include Cronbach's $\alpha$ \citet{Cronbach}, J{\"o}reskog's congeneric reliability $\rho_C$ \citet{Joereskog}, and Dijkstra--Henseler's $\rho_A$ \citet{Dijkstra}.
These coefficients differ in their assumption imposed on the measurement models.
Cronbach's $\alpha$ assumes equal weights and tau-equivalent items, i.e., equal population loadings across indicators \citet{}.
J{\"o}reskog's $\rho_C$ allows loadings to differ across indicators but still assumes unit weights when forming the construct scores \citet{}.
Dijkstra--Henseler's $\rho_A$ relaxes both, as it is based on the estimated weights, and thus matches how the construct scores were actually computed \citet{}.

We can extract the information for the reliability assessment out of the assess object with the \code{\$} operator: 
<<assess-rho-A>>=
# Show reliability criteria
quality$Reliability
@

Additionally the \fn{assess()} function offers alternative reliability coefficients. 
<<assess-rho_C>>=
# Show alternative reliability coefficients.
quality$RhoC
quality$RhoC_mm
quality$RhoC_weighted
quality$RhoC_weighted_mm
@  


\subsubsection*{Evaluating Indicator Reliability}
While the reliability of the construct scores summarizes the measurement quality of each construct as a whole, indicator reliability examines the contribution of each individual indicator. 
Indicator reliability assesses how much variance can be explained by the underlying construct.
It can be assessed via the factor loadings, since the squared standardized loading equals the proportion of indicator variance attributable to the construct.
For that we extract out of the \fn{csem()} object, \code{res\_csem}, the loadings, its standard errors, and its corresponding confidence intervals.
To also judge the precision and significance of the loading estimates, standard errors and confidence intervals are needed, which in PLS-PM are obtained by resampling. 
This is why bootstrap was already requested in the \fn{csem()} call in Section \ref{sec:estimation}.

<<loadings-boot>>=
# Loading estimates incl. bootstrap std. errors, t-values and p-values
summarize(res_csem)$Estimates$Loading_estimates
@

\subsubsection*{Evaluating Convergent Validity}
Convergent validity is the extent to which the indicators belonging to one latent variable actually measure the same construct \citet{}.
In the \pkg{cSEM} package this is done with the average variance extracted (AVE) \citet{AVE}.
The AVE summarizes, per construct, how much of the indicators' variance is captured by the construct rather than by measurement error \citet{}. 
It thus aggregates the indicator reliabilities from the previous step to the construct level.

<<ave>>=
# Average variance extracted (AVE)
quality$AVE
@

\subsubsection*{Evaluating Discriminant Validity}
Discriminant validity refers to the extent to which latent variables are sufficiently distinct from one another to model them as distinct constructs.
The \pkg{cSEM} package assesses discriminant validity using the HTMT and its improved version, the HTMT2.
Both are calculated with the default options within the \fn{assess()} function.
To conduct inference, the \prm{.inference} argument can be set to \code{"bootstrap"} or \code{"asymptotic"}, in which case the corresponding confidence intervals are also computed.
When performing inference, it is recommended to set \prm{.absolute} to \code{FALSE}.
%Discriminant validity is considered established if the upper limit of the confidence interval lies below the chosen threshold.

<<htmt, warning = TRUE>>=
# Heterotrait-monotrait ratio of correlations (HTMT) 
quality$HTMT

# Heterotrait-monotrait ratio of correlations (HTMT) with inference
HTMT <- assess(res_csem, 
               .quality_criterion = "htmt", 
               .inference = "asymptotic", 
               .absolute = FALSE)
HTMT
@

The warning message says that some of the correlations between the indicators have different signs.
This does not lead to any calculation issues.

The improved version HTMT2 and its inference can be calculated via bootstrap confidence intervals. 
<<htmt2, warning = FALSE>>=
# Heterotrait-monotrait ratio 2 of correlations (HTMT2)
quality$HTMT2

# Heterotrait-monotrait ratio 2 of correlations (HTMT2) with inference
HTMT2 <- assess(res_csem,
                .quality_criterion = "htmt2",
                .inference = "bootstrap",
                .absolute = FALSE, 
                .seed = 42)
HTMT2
@


\subsection{Structural Model Evaluation}
\label{sec:structural}

%direct indirect effects  fehlt noch

The final part of the assessment concerns the structural model.
First, the path coefficients are inspected with respect to sign, magnitude, and significance, where the inference is based on resample methods, such as bootstrap. 
Second, the contribution of each predictor is quantified by the effect size $f^2$.
Third, the explained variance of the endogenous constructs is summarized by $R^2$ and its adjusted version.

<<paths-boot>>=
# Path coefficient estimates incl. bootstrap std. errors, t-values and p-values
summarize(res_csem)$Estimates$Path_estimates
@


To judge the relevance of each individual path, the effect size $f^2$ can be consulted. 
It measures the increase in unexplained variance that results from dropping a predictor, relative to the unexplained variance of the full model, and thereby isolates each predictor's contribution.

<<effect-sizes>>=
# Effect sizes (f^2)
quality$F2
@


As a last step, the explained variance of the endogenous constructs is examined.
For each of \glqq JS\grqq, \glqq OC\grqq, and \glqq SI\grqq, $R^2$ gives the proportion of variance accounted for by the structural equation; the adjusted $R^2$ penalizes for the number of predictors and thus allows a fairer comparison across equations of different size.

<<r-squared>>=
# Coefficient of determination (R^2) and adjusted R^2
quality$R2
quality$R2_adj
@


\section{Further features of \texorpdfstring{\pkg{cSEM}}{cSEM}}
\label{sec:further}
\subsection{Customization of PLS-PM}

<<custom-overview>>=
# All PLS-PM arguments of csem() at their defaults
res_default <- csem(
  .data                        = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model                       = model,
  # Weight estimation: outer and inner weights
  .PLS_modes                   = NULL,              # "modeA", "modeB", ...
  .PLS_weight_scheme_inner     = "path",            # "centroid", "factorial"
  .PLS_ignore_structural_model = FALSE,
  .starting_values             = NULL,
  .dominant_indicators         = NULL,
  # Weight estimation: convergence
  .conv_criterion              = "diff_absolute",   # "diff_squared", "diff_relative"
  .tolerance                   = 1e-05,
  .iter_max                    = 100,
  # Correction for attenuation (PLSc)
  .disattenuate                = TRUE,
  .PLS_approach_cf             = "dist_squared_euclid",
  .reliabilities               = NULL,
  # Structural model
  .approach_paths              = "OLS",             # "2SLS"
  .approach_nl                 = "sequential",      # "replace"
  .normality                   = FALSE,
  .id                          = NULL
)
@
\label{sec:customization}

% TODO (Jason): lead-in. csem() runs with defaults; every default is an
% argument of csem(). Link back to Section \ref{sec:overall-fit}: a failed
% verify() check is the typical reason to change one of them. The arguments
% are grouped in the order in which csem() uses them: (1) outer and inner
% weights, (2) convergence of the iteration, (3) correction for attenuation,
% (4) estimation of the structural model. Each block opens with a chunk that
% shows its arguments at their defaults (all four reproduce res_csem without
% bootstrap, verified 2026-10-14), followed by one example that changes a
% default.

The function \fn{csem()} has a lot of input arguments.
These input arguments shape the PLS argument in four different dimensions. 
First the outer and inner weights. 
Second, the convergence of the algorithm.
Third, the correction for attenuation.
Fourth, the estimation of the strutural model.
All of these input arguments have default options, which should be best for most cases. 
A failed \fn{verify()} check is a reason to adapt the algorithm.
In the following most input parameters are presented with their default options, followed by one example that changes a default. 

\subsubsection*{Outer and Inner Weights}

% TODO (Jason): how the composites are formed. \prm{.PLS\_modes} sets the
% outer mode per construct (default NULL = Mode A for common factors, Mode B
% for composites); \prm{.PLS\_weight\_scheme\_inner} the inner scheme;
% \prm{.PLS\_ignore\_structural\_model} = TRUE drops the structural model
% from the inner weighting; \prm{.starting\_values} replaces the default
% starting weights; \prm{.dominant\_indicators} fixes the sign of the weights
% so that each construct correlates positively with its dominant indicator.
% Mode B via .PLS_modes deliberately not shown: on this model it yields
% reliabilities > 1 and verify() flags it (TODO.md).



<<custom-weights-default>>=
# Weight estimation: arguments at their defaults
res_weights <- csem(
  .data                        = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model                       = model,
  .PLS_modes                   = NULL,     # "modeA", "modeB", "modeBNNLS",
                                           # "unit", "PCA" or fixed weights
  .PLS_weight_scheme_inner     = "path",   # "centroid", "factorial"
  .PLS_ignore_structural_model = FALSE,
  .starting_values             = NULL,
  .dominant_indicators         = NULL
)
@

% TODO (Jason): example. Switch to the centroid scheme; path estimates barely
% move (verified 2026-10-13).

<<custom-weighting>>=
# Re-estimate with the centroid inner weighting scheme
res_centroid <- csem(
  .data                    = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model                   = model,
  .PLS_weight_scheme_inner = "centroid"
)
res_centroid$Estimates$Path_estimates
@

\subsubsection*{Convergence}

% TODO (Jason): the algorithm iterates between inner and outer weights until
% the change in the weights, measured by \prm{.conv\_criterion}, falls below
% \prm{.tolerance} or \prm{.iter\_max} iterations are reached. If verify()
% check 1 fails: raise \prm{.iter\_max}, loosen \prm{.tolerance}, or change
% the weighting scheme (previous block). \prm{.iter\_max} = 1 returns
% one-step weights.

<<custom-convergence-default>>=
# Convergence: arguments at their defaults
res_conv <- csem(
  .data           = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model          = model,
  .conv_criterion = "diff_absolute",   # "diff_squared", "diff_relative"
  .tolerance      = 1e-05,
  .iter_max       = 100
)
@

% TODO (Jason): example. Capping the iterations at two forces a failed
% convergence check, so the reader sees what "not ok" in verify() looks like
% (verified 2026-10-14: check 1 not ok, checks 2-5 ok).

<<custom-nonconvergence>>=
# Force non-convergence by capping the iterations
res_cap <- csem(
  .data     = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model    = model,
  .iter_max = 2
)
verify(res_cap)
@

\subsubsection*{Correction for Attenuation (PLSc)}

% TODO (Jason): by default csem() applies the PLSc correction for attenuation
% to all constructs specified as common factors (=~). \prm{.PLS\_approach\_cf}
% selects how the correction factors are obtained; the seven options are
% "dist_squared_euclid" (default), "dist_euclid_weighted",
% "fisher_transformed", "mean_arithmetic", "mean_geometric",
% "mean_harmonic", "geo_of_harmonic" (too many for a code comment, list them
% in the prose). \prm{.reliabilities} lets the user supply known
% reliabilities ("construct" = value) for some or all constructs instead of
% estimating them.

<<custom-plsc-default>>=
# Correction for attenuation: arguments at their defaults
res_plsc <- csem(
  .data            = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model           = model,
  .disattenuate    = TRUE,
  .PLS_approach_cf = "dist_squared_euclid",
  .reliabilities   = NULL                  # e.g. c("AC" = 0.85)
)
@

% TODO (Jason): example. \prm{.disattenuate} = FALSE switches the correction
% off and gives the traditional PLS-PM estimates (what SmartPLS reports as
% PLS-SEM). On this data the correction matters: EP -> OC 0.38 vs. 0.43,
% OC -> SI 0.44 vs. 0.49 (verified 2026-10-13). Keep it short here; the full
% comparison with CB-SEM is Section 4.

<<custom-disattenuate>>=
# Traditional PLS-PM: no correction for attenuation
res_pls <- csem(
  .data         = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model        = model,
  .disattenuate = FALSE
)
res_pls$Estimates$Path_estimates
@

\subsubsection*{Structural Model}

% TODO (Jason): once the weights are fixed, the structural coefficients are
% estimated. \prm{.approach\_paths}: OLS by default, "2SLS" requires
% instruments via \prm{.instruments}. \prm{.approach\_nl} and
% \prm{.normality} only matter for nonlinear models (ignored otherwise) --
% one sentence each plus a forward reference to Section 5.3. \prm{.id} names
% the column that splits the data into groups -- one sentence plus a forward
% reference to Section 5.4 (MGA). No example chunk in this block: 2SLS needs
% instruments the model does not have, the other three belong to 5.3/5.4.

<<custom-structural-default>>=
# Structural model: arguments at their defaults
res_struct <- csem(
  .data           = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model          = model,
  .approach_paths = "OLS",          # "2SLS" (needs .instruments)
  .approach_nl    = "sequential",   # "replace"; nonlinear models only
  .normality      = FALSE,          # nonlinear models only
  .id             = NULL            # grouping variable, see Section 5.4
)
@

